    "reading": {
        "body": "Atlassian embeds AI into collaborative work tools rather than selling standalone AI products, with $5.2 billion in annual revenue from a platform spanning project management, documentation, and team coordination.\n\nThe company's AI strategy targets the workflow layer where teams already operate. Confluence AI and Trello AI do not compete with foundation models or inference providers. Instead, they add AI capabilities to existing products used for planning and tracking work, positioning automation and intelligence as features within established collaboration surfaces. This reflects a broader pattern: companies with installed bases in enterprise workflow are adding AI as a capability layer rather than rebuilding their platforms around it.\n\nFor compute and infrastructure, this model differs significantly from training-focused AI companies. Atlassian's AI products likely consume inference capacity at scale but do not require the capital-intensive building and power investments that training or frontier models demand. The revenue scale, however, means the inference load across their user base could be substantial. The data does not reveal Atlassian's inference architecture, cloud provider choices, or compute spend, only that both products operate under Atlassian Cloud governance.\n\nThe products also signal what remains unsolved: team data exposure is noted as a feature of Trello AI, suggesting that AI-driven insights into internal work requires explicit governance and user control rather than automatic integration. This indicates enterprise AI adoption in workflow tools is proceeding with friction rather than seamlessly.",
